From d950a85c6102a4d74ebe2926c8fbf921785384ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 15:35:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/11] Delve: say what "per delve" means, and stop overstating
 it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two problems with the headline number, both mine.

The default node frequencies were too generous. exclusivePerDelve was
0.35 — a Crystal Spire every three delves, about one Hollow Fossil per
delve — and the economy refutes that on its own: Hollow would not hold
above a divine if delvers pulled one a run. Dropped to 0.1, with the
generic node and cache rates cut to match. Every per-delve knob now
prints what it implies in plain terms ("about 1 every 10 delves") next
to the field and in the breakdown, so a bad setting announces itself
instead of quietly tripling a biome's value.

And the unit was never defined on the page. "389c per delve" reads as
"a delve is worth 389c"; it isn't, it is the FOSSILS in one run between
checkpoints, off assumed node counts, with no rares, currency, azurite
or sulphite in it. That now sits in a strip under the toolbar with a
link into the assumptions, rather than in a tooltip.

Also: the biome panel no longer renders a 260px empty box before any
history exists, which is every biome on a fresh snapshot.

